# CITATION.cff: how to cite StrainScope (Phase 8).
# GitHub reads this file and shows a "Cite this repository" button on the
# project's page, with the reference ready to copy (APA or BibTeX).
# The version and date move with every release (docs/09-packaging.md).

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use StrainScope or build on it, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "StrainScope: multi-omics screening of beneficial microbes, explained"
abstract: >-
  StrainScope reads the genes and the chemistry of beneficial microbes
  (bacteria, fungi, yeasts and oomycetes that protect crops) together, to
  predict which strains are worth developing and to explain why. It covers
  multi-omics integration (DIABLO in R with a Python twin), leak-proof machine
  learning with calibrated probabilities, a knowledge graph that explains
  every ranking, a Streamlit app with assistants that answer from the
  project's own documents, data and graph, and an MCP server for AI assistant
  apps. The strain data is simulated by a seeded generator, which every
  document says; real records from five public databases can be fetched
  alongside it.
authors:
  - family-names: Kannan
    given-names: Abhilash
version: 1.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-13"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/akannan2987/strainscope"
url: "https://github.com/akannan2987/strainscope"
keywords:
  - multi-omics
  - beneficial microbes
  - biocontrol
  - machine learning
  - knowledge graph
  - DIABLO
  - Streamlit
  - Model Context Protocol
